{
  "title": "The fourth condition of Salah – praying on time",
  "pages": "45",
  "intro": "Every prayer has its own time – five times, five prayers.",
  "learn": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "html": "The <b>fourth condition</b> of Salah is to pray each prayer in its <b>set time</b>. There are five prayer times – as many as the prayers:"
    },
    {
      "type": "cards",
      "items": [
        {
          "icon": "🌅",
          "label": "Fajr – the morning prayer"
        },
        {
          "icon": "☀️",
          "label": "Dhuhr – the midday prayer"
        },
        {
          "icon": "🌤️",
          "label": "Asr – the afternoon prayer"
        },
        {
          "icon": "🌇",
          "label": "Maghrib – the evening prayer"
        },
        {
          "icon": "🌙",
          "label": "Isha – the night prayer"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "We cannot pray:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "while the sun is rising",
        "while the sun is in the middle of the sky",
        "while the sun is setting"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "It is disliked (makruh) to pray (roughly):"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "about 45 minutes after sunrise",
        "5–10 minutes while the sun is at its highest",
        "about 45 minutes before sunset – except that day’s Asr"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "point",
      "title": "⭐ Remember",
      "html": "The start of each prayer time is announced by the <b>muezzin</b> with the adhan, at the exact time in the yearly Islamic calendar – the prayer timetable."
    }
  ],
